<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aratus of Soli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 315–c. 240 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aratus of Soli was a Greek poet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> who lived at the Macedonian court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaenomena">Phaenomena</a>, a didactic poem describing the constellations and weather signs, became one of the most widely read works of ancient literature, translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> and quoted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a>. Hipparchus wrote a commentary criticising its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aratus-of-soli.jpg" alt="Aratus of Soli" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aratos_von_Soloi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
